WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:05.072
Hello, everyone! I'm Ms. Emma. Welcome to Online C two, Book one.

2
00:00:05.372 --> 00:00:08.816
Hi! I'm Visal. Let's learn together!

3
00:00:09.116 --> 00:00:16.145
Unit twenty-five: Leading Innovation. Today you will express counterfactual dependence with Were it not for….

4
00:00:16.445 --> 00:00:19.132
Here is our lesson map. Let's start!

5
00:00:20.067 --> 00:00:23.427
Part one: words. You will learn eight new words.

6
00:00:24.283 --> 00:00:25.366
Innovation.

7
00:00:26.350 --> 00:00:30.370
Innovation means the introduction of new ideas, methods or products.

8
00:00:30.983 --> 00:00:34.550
Innovation in mobile payments transformed Cambodian retail.

9
00:00:35.150 --> 00:00:37.768
Your turn. Say: Innovation.

10
00:00:40.633 --> 00:00:41.646
Innovation.

11
00:00:42.383 --> 00:00:43.814
Experimentation.

12
00:00:44.817 --> 00:00:49.092
Experimentation means the process of testing ideas to learn what works.

13
00:00:49.683 --> 00:00:52.809
Constant experimentation kept the company ahead.

14
00:00:53.417 --> 00:00:56.533
Your turn. Say: Experimentation.

15
00:00:59.400 --> 00:01:00.982
Experimentation.

16
00:01:01.717 --> 00:01:02.544
Failure.

17
00:01:03.550 --> 00:01:06.351
Failure means lack of success in achieving an aim.

18
00:01:06.950 --> 00:01:09.635
Every failure revealed a flawed assumption.

19
00:01:10.250 --> 00:01:12.798
Your turn. Say: Failure.

20
00:01:15.633 --> 00:01:16.611
Failure.

21
00:01:17.350 --> 00:01:18.537
Creativity.

22
00:01:19.550 --> 00:01:22.769
Creativity means the ability to produce original ideas.

23
00:01:23.383 --> 00:01:26.288
Creativity flourishes when people feel safe to fail.

24
00:01:26.883 --> 00:01:29.547
Your turn. Say: Creativity.

25
00:01:32.400 --> 00:01:33.448
Creativity.

26
00:01:34.183 --> 00:01:35.184
Disruption.

27
00:01:36.183 --> 00:01:39.994
Disruption means radical change that transforms an industry.

28
00:01:40.583 --> 00:01:43.442
Digital disruption reshaped banking within a decade.

29
00:01:44.050 --> 00:01:46.691
Your turn. Say: Disruption.

30
00:01:49.533 --> 00:01:50.778
Disruption.

31
00:01:51.517 --> 00:01:52.541
Prototype.

32
00:01:53.550 --> 00:01:56.861
Prototype means an early model built to test a concept.

33
00:01:57.450 --> 00:01:59.868
The first prototype was made of cardboard.

34
00:02:00.483 --> 00:02:03.205
Your turn. Say: Prototype.

35
00:02:06.067 --> 00:02:07.103
Prototype.

36
00:02:07.850 --> 00:02:09.014
Iteration.

37
00:02:10.017 --> 00:02:12.864
Iteration means a repeated cycle of improvement.

38
00:02:13.450 --> 00:02:15.740
Each iteration made the app simpler.

39
00:02:16.350 --> 00:02:19.304
Your turn. Say: Iteration.

40
00:02:22.167 --> 00:02:23.330
Iteration.

41
00:02:24.083 --> 00:02:24.887
Vision.

42
00:02:25.883 --> 00:02:29.485
Vision means a clear, inspiring idea of the future.

43
00:02:30.083 --> 00:02:32.663
Vision gives experimentation a direction.

44
00:02:33.250 --> 00:02:35.438
Your turn. Say: Vision.

45
00:02:38.300 --> 00:02:39.162
Vision.

46
00:02:39.967 --> 00:02:43.478
Part two: dialogue. Let's listen to a conversation.

47
00:02:44.317 --> 00:02:50.998
Visal, the team's first three prototypes of a solar rice dryer failed completely. Should the company cancel the project?

48
00:02:52.083 --> 00:03:01.353
Not necessarily. Were it not for those failures, the engineers wouldn't have discovered that humidity, not heat, was the real problem.

49
00:03:02.450 --> 00:03:04.194
But failure is expensive.

50
00:03:05.283 --> 00:03:17.238
Large failures are. That's why innovative leaders encourage small, cheap experiments. Were it not for such tolerance, few people would dare propose bold ideas.

51
00:03:18.350 --> 00:03:19.827
How do you know when to stop?

52
00:03:20.917 --> 00:03:29.734
When experiments stop producing learning. Failure is valuable only if it informs the next iteration rather than repeating the last.

53
00:03:30.900 --> 00:03:32.319
Part three: grammar.

54
00:03:33.183 --> 00:03:36.750
Grammar focus: Were it not for (inverted conditional).

55
00:03:37.050 --> 00:03:38.249
Look at the pattern.

56
00:03:38.950 --> 00:03:42.215
Were it not for failure, few innovations would succeed.

57
00:03:43.117 --> 00:03:47.531
Were it not for tolerance of risk, few people would propose bold ideas.

58
00:03:48.417 --> 00:03:52.227
Had it not been for the third prototype, we would never have found the flaw.

59
00:03:53.167 --> 00:04:02.613
'Were it not for + noun' = 'If it were not for + noun' (present/general). For the past, use 'Had it not been for + noun' (= If it hadn't been for). Never omit 'for'.

60
00:04:03.350 --> 00:04:07.605
Be careful! Don't say: Were it not failure, few succeed.

61
00:04:09.567 --> 00:04:12.588
Say: Were it not for failure, few would succeed.

62
00:04:12.888 --> 00:04:14.458
Were it not for + noun.

63
00:04:15.267 --> 00:04:17.756
Part four: reading. Read and listen.

64
00:04:18.567 --> 00:04:20.218
The Productive Failure.

65
00:04:20.518 --> 00:04:27.672
Innovation is commonly celebrated for its successes, yet historians of technology emphasise the countless failures that preceded them.

66
00:04:28.667 --> 00:04:35.984
Thomas Edison reportedly tested thousands of materials before finding a workable filament, treating each failure as information.

67
00:04:37.000 --> 00:04:43.028
Were it not for organisational tolerance of such experimentation, many breakthroughs would have been abandoned prematurely.

68
00:04:44.033 --> 00:04:52.082
Leaders of innovation therefore distinguish between intelligent failures, which generate learning, and careless failures, which result from avoidable mistakes.

69
00:04:53.067 --> 00:05:00.662
By encouraging rapid prototyping and honest reflection, they create cultures in which creativity and discipline reinforce each other.

70
00:05:01.767 --> 00:05:05.838
Part five: quiz. Five questions. Are you ready?

71
00:05:06.683 --> 00:05:08.996
Were it not blank your help, I would fail.

72
00:05:09.296 --> 00:05:11.951
A, for. B, of. C, to.

73
00:05:15.500 --> 00:05:17.116
The answer is A: for.

74
00:05:18.250 --> 00:05:21.387
An early model built to test a concept is a blank.

75
00:05:21.687 --> 00:05:25.434
A, prototype. B, disruption. C, vision.

76
00:05:29.000 --> 00:05:31.185
The answer is A: prototype.

77
00:05:32.350 --> 00:05:35.569
Radical change that transforms an industry is blank.

78
00:05:35.869 --> 00:05:39.801
A, iteration. B, creativity. C, disruption.

79
00:05:43.367 --> 00:05:45.227
The answer is C: disruption.

80
00:05:46.383 --> 00:05:48.151
Choose the correct sentence.

81
00:05:48.451 --> 00:05:55.843
A, Had it not been for the rain, we would have won.. B, Had it not for the rain, we would win.. C, Were it not the rain, we would have won..

82
00:05:59.400 --> 00:06:03.013
The answer is A: Had it not been for the rain, we would have won..

83
00:06:04.150 --> 00:06:06.846
According to the reading, intelligent failures…

84
00:06:07.146 --> 00:06:12.123
A, result from carelessness. B, generate learning. C, should be hidden.

85
00:06:15.667 --> 00:06:18.096
The answer is B: generate learning.

86
00:06:19.300 --> 00:06:22.498
Part six: speaking. It's your turn to talk.

87
00:06:23.350 --> 00:06:25.605
Why is failure important for innovation?

88
00:06:42.417 --> 00:06:43.836
Now listen to Visal.

89
00:06:44.136 --> 00:07:19.594
Failure is important because innovation means exploring the unknown, and the unknown cannot be mapped without wrong turns. Each failed experiment eliminates a false assumption and narrows the path towards a workable solution. Were it not for this process, organisations would only repeat what already works, and genuine breakthroughs would be rare. However, failure is valuable only when it is small, fast and analysed honestly. Leaders must therefore make it safe to fail intelligently.

90
00:07:19.894 --> 00:07:21.661
Excellent answer, Visal!

91
00:07:22.550 --> 00:07:27.188
Great work! Now you can use eight new words and were it not for (inverted conditional).

92
00:07:27.488 --> 00:07:32.717
Today's value is innovation. Try new ideas boldly, and learn from every attempt.

93
00:07:33.017 --> 00:07:35.937
See you in Unit twenty-six. Goodbye!

94
00:07:36.237 --> 00:07:39.272
Thank you, Ms. Emma! Bye, everyone!

